the held-out centre are exchangeable (within strata) and block \code{k} is
independent of the other blocks there, whatever the training data, the
tuning or the sparsity. Two conditions on the preprocessing make this hold:
\itemize{
\item The held-out scores of block \code{k} must depend only on block \code{k}'s data.
Impute within blocks (\code{impute_within_blocks = TRUE} in
\code{\link[=mbspls_graph_learner]{mbspls_graph_learner()}}, or \code{\link[=impute_knn_graph]{impute_knn_graph()}} with \code{blocks}) and keep
covariate corrections within blocks.
\item Any centring of the held-out data must be constant within the centre. The
statistic is invariant to it, so centre effects that shift a block's mean
do not create false participation.
}

The p-values of different centres are dependent, because the training sets
overlap. Moreover, whether block \code{k} participates at centre \code{s} depends on
the weights learned at the other centres, so the set of centres at which
the null hypothesis holds is itself random. Two bounds are reported
(\code{\link[=mbspls_partial_conjunction]{mbspls_partial_conjunction()}}):
\itemize{
\item \code{r_count} is the number of centres with \code{p <= alpha / S}. It is a lower
\code{1 - alpha} confidence bound for the number of centres at which the block
participates in the rule fitted without that centre, whatever the
dependence between centres and although the hypotheses depend on the
data: counting a centre wrongly requires a true-null p-value at most the
fixed threshold \code{alpha / S}, which has conditional probability at most
\code{alpha / S} at each centre. \code{r_count_simultaneous} uses \verb{alpha / (K S)}
for \code{K} blocks, so that the counts of all blocks hold jointly.
\item \code{r_hat} is the Bonferroni partial conjunction bound, the largest \code{r}
whose partial conjunction p-value
\verb{max_\{r' <= r\} min(1, (S - r' + 1) p_(r'))} is at most \code{alpha};
\code{r_hat_simultaneous} uses \code{alpha / K}. It is never smaller than
\code{r_count} and is valid under arbitrary dependence when the null
hypotheses are fixed in advance: for example when one frozen signature
is tested at independent centres, or for the number of centres at which
block \code{k} is not independent of the other blocks at all (which implies
non-participation for every fitted rule). For the participation of rules
learned at the other centres it can exceed its target with probability
above \code{alpha}, because two centres can make each other's null hypotheses
false.
